Subject: [PATCH] qt6 QPA genode: pace frames against the compositor

Register the Genode framebuffer sync signal in the Qt6 'genode' QPA
platform plugin and wait (bounded) for it at each frame boundary, so a
client can no longer queue repaints faster than the compositor drains
them.

Background
----------
A Genode GUI client owns exactly ONE framebuffer dataspace, mapped once;
'Framebuffer::Session' has no release or rotation API. 'refresh()' only
marks a rectangle dirty. The server composites on its own clock -
nitpicker throttles to one frame per 40 ms
(genode/repos/os/src/server/nitpicker/main.cc:561-564) - and submits one
framebuffer sync signal per composited frame
(genode/repos/os/src/server/nitpicker/main.cc:537).

The QPA plugin never registered that sync signal, so the client had no
way to learn how far behind the compositor was. Under sustained
repaints the damage backlog grows without bound while nothing leaks:
measured on Sponge OS, 335 full-document viewport repaints over 30 s
moved ZERO bytes in every component of the render chain, while the
stack delivered 11 repaints/s against a requested 30
(docs/evidence/sponge-debug-scrollstorm.md). Input therefore arrived
~3x faster than frames could be produced and the event loop stopped
draining - the field symptom "dragging the scrollbar freezes the
system".

Fix
---
  qgenodeplatformwindow.h   frame-pacing state, the sync signal
                            handler, and the public wait_for_frame()
  qgenodeplatformwindow.cpp sync_sigh registration, the counter
                            handler and the bounded wait
  qgenodewindowsurface.cpp  one wait per raster frame (flush())
  qgenodeglcontext.cpp      one wait per GL frame (swapBuffers())

Properties that matter for safety:

  * BOUNDED. The wait is 50 steps x 5 ms = 250 ms maximum per frame and
    then proceeds unpaced, re-arming for the next frame. A missed or
    stopped sync signal degrades to the old behaviour; it can never hang
    the application.

  * SELF-DISABLING, AND ZERO-COST WHEN THERE IS NOTHING TO WAIT FOR.
    The test is exact and needs no tuning threshold: a frame is still
    in flight precisely when the client submits a new one without a
    sync having arrived since its last submit. A client that keeps up
    always sees the compositor's sync first and never waits - not even
    a poll. A server that never submits syncs at all (a capture-less
    headless topology) leaves every count untouched and is likewise
    never waited on. A client that outruns the compositor submits
    again first, and that is the one case that waits.

    Two earlier revisions were measured and rejected: waiting on every
    frame after observing two syncs cost 2 repaints/s (10 Hz -> 8 Hz),
    and a per-interval "more than one refresh in one compositor
    interval" heuristic cost the same, because a client painting at
    ~10 Hz against a ~25 Hz compositor produces two refreshes per
    interval often enough to trigger it roughly every other frame. Only
    the exact test is free. Receipts: docs/evidence/
    sponge-debug-scrollstorm.md.

  * THREADING. The Genode signal handler runs on the component
    entrypoint and does nothing but a counter increment under a lock -
    no Qt, no blocking. The Qt thread reads the counter inside
    wait_for_frame(). The wait therefore blocks the Qt event loop for at
    most one compositor frame, which is exactly what a vsync-locked
    application does; the Genode entrypoint is never blocked.

  * MINIMAL. No behaviour outside the frame-submit path is touched.

Measured effect and regression receipts: docs/evidence/sponge-debug-
scrollstorm.md; ledger row #23 in docs/11-environment.md section 4.

Drop when upstream Genode's qt6 port registers the framebuffer sync in
the QPA platform plugin (or replaces 'refresh()' with a vsync-paced
submit).

